<SanitizeUserPrompt name="SUP-SanitizeUserPrompt" continueOnError="true" enabled="true">
  <DisplayName>Sanitize User Prompt (Model Armor)</DisplayName>
  <!--
    continueOnError="true" since 2026-09-15 (was "false"). This REVERSES the
    fail-closed posture this policy was built with, and it is a deliberate
    decision, not a default: a Model Armor outage or a RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED on
    its 1,200 QPM project quota previously took down real user LLM calls.

    Read RF-PromptBlocked.xml before touching this attribute. On its own this
    change would ALSO swallow steps.sanitize.user.prompt.FilterMatched — the
    fault Apigee raises for a genuine prompt-injection detection — which would
    quietly demote Model Armor from an enforcement control to a logging one.
    RF-PromptBlocked re-raises that block from the verdict variable and is the
    only thing preventing that. The two must be deployed and removed together.

    What this now means operationally:
      Model Armor says MATCH_FOUND   -> RF-PromptBlocked returns 400 (enforced)
      Model Armor unreachable/quota  -> call proceeds UNSCANNED, and the
                                        ma_prompt_failed field in the
                                        llm-gateway-attribution log is the only
                                        record that safety coverage was lost.
    That second line is a real reduction in security posture, accepted
    knowingly. Alert on ma_prompt_failed — an unscanned call is invisible
    otherwise.
  -->
  <Source>request</Source>
  <!--
    Explicit because the policy's DEFAULT expression resolves to nothing here
    and that is fatal, not a skip:

      500 steps.sanitize.user.prompt.FailedToExtractUserPrompt
      "Failed to evaluate user prompt
       {jsonPath($.contents[-1].parts[-1].text,request.content)}.
       Reason: Template was resolved to empty string"

    seen live 2026-09-15 on the first call that ever reached Model Armor.
    The default uses NEGATIVE array indices to take the most recent turn;
    this expression uses [0] instead, which is equivalent for this gateway's
    traffic because every call it carries is single-turn. The request body
    google-genai builds is
      {"contents":[{"role":"user","parts":[{"text": "..."}]}], ...}
    If a multi-turn caller is ever added, revisit this: [0] would scan the
    OLDEST turn rather than the newest, which would still scan something (so
    no error) but would be scanning the wrong text. That is the failure mode
    to watch for here, and it is silent.
  -->
  <UserPromptSource>{llm.prompt_text}</UserPromptSource>
  <ModelArmor>
    <!--
      This literal path must equal the model_armor_template_id output of
      terraform/7-apigee-runtime/model-armor.tf. A policy cannot read a
      Terraform output, so the two are coupled by convention only: if the
      template's project, region or id changes there, this line and
      SMR-SanitizeModelResponse.xml change with it.

      Pointing at a template that does not exist fails CLOSED — a policy error
      on every single call, not a silent skip. That is the desired direction,
      but it means this proxy must not be deployed before the Terraform apply.
    -->
    <TemplateName>projects/gclt-aicoe-dev-llm/locations/europe-west3/templates/aicoe-llm-gateway</TemplateName>
  </ModelArmor>
</SanitizeUserPrompt>
